see "../src/harness.vox".
see "../src/runner.vox".
see "../src/rng.vox".
see "../src/gen_literals.vox".

(Every helper in src/gen_literals.vox, put on trial three ways.

 1. LEGALITY, by the real compiler. Each helper is drawn 120 times and
    each draw is dropped into the declaration its kind belongs in - an
    integer into `a number called`, a float into `a float called`, a
    character into `a number called` (LANGUAGE.md:1870 says 'A' equals
    65), `nothing` into a list slot (:2661 is explicit that a list slot
    is one of the three positions it may occupy). That program is then
    compiled AND run by the compiler under test. Checking the emitted
    text by shape would prove nothing here: the whole question is
    whether the compiler accepts these tokens, so the compiler has to
    answer it.

    The format strings are the one exception, and the reason is
    measured, not assumed. A format hole's width is a literal and
    LANGUAGE.md bounds it nowhere, so 'literal format specifier' draws it
    unbounded like every other literal - and `Print "{n:1000000000}"`
    renders a billion spaces, which on this machine did not finish in
    twenty seconds. So the format strings go into a function that is
    never called: an uncalled body is still fully compile-checked
    (verified - an unknown name inside one is still "Unknown variable"),
    which is exactly the legality question, while nothing renders a
    gigabyte at run time. What a wide pad DOES at run time is a
    campaign's business, not this test's.

 2. LENGTH DISTRIBUTION, measured and printed. The numbers below are a
    fingerprint of seed 4242 under the knob in gen_literals.vox, NOT a
    bound: nothing here asserts a maximum, and nothing may. The two
    assertions are the two rules - every literal is at least one byte
    long, and lengths actually vary - plus the evidence the tail is real
    (a draw longer than twelve bytes does occur).

 3. DETERMINISM. Same seed, same literals - the property every finding's
    reproducibility rests on.)

a buffer called 'the corpus' is "".
a buffer called 'the sample tape' is "".

a number called 'the shortest' is 0.
a number called 'the longest' is 0.
a number called 'the total length' is 0.
a number called 'how many measured' is 0.
a number called 'how many over twelve' is 0.

To 'put' with a text called 'the line'.
    a buffer called 'the piece' is "{'the line'}".
    append 'the piece' to 'the corpus'.

To 'length in bytes' with a text called 'the literal'.
    a buffer called 'the bytes' is "{'the literal'}".
    Return a number, 'the bytes's length.

To measure with a text called 'the literal'.
    a number called 'the length' is 'length in bytes' of 'the literal'.
    If 'how many measured' is 0 then,
        Set 'the shortest' to 'the length'.
    If 'the length' is less than 'the shortest' then,
        Set 'the shortest' to 'the length'.
    If 'the length' is greater than 'the longest' then,
        Set 'the longest' to 'the length'.
    If 'the length' is greater than 12 then,
        Set 'how many over twelve' to 'how many over twelve' add 1.
    Set 'the total length' to 'the total length' add 'the length'.
    Set 'how many measured' to 'how many measured' add 1.

(Every draw also goes onto a tape, so the determinism check compares the
 literals themselves and not merely the program built from them.)

To 'draw and record' with a text called 'the literal'.
    measure of 'the literal'.
    a buffer called 'the piece' is "{'the literal'}\n".
    append 'the piece' to 'the sample tape'.
    Return a text, 'the literal'.

To 'one round of every helper' with a number called 'the round'.
    a text called 'the integer' is 'draw and record' of 'literal integer'.
    'put' of "a number called n{'the round'} is {'the integer'}.\n".
    a text called 'the whole number' is 'draw and record' of 'literal whole number'.
    'put' of "a number called u{'the round'} is {'the whole number'}.\n".
    a text called 'the float' is 'draw and record' of 'literal float'.
    'put' of "a float called g{'the round'} is {'the float'}.\n".
    a text called 'the hexadecimal' is 'draw and record' of 'literal hexadecimal'.
    'put' of "a number called h{'the round'} is {'the hexadecimal'}.\n".
    a text called 'the binary' is 'draw and record' of 'literal binary'.
    'put' of "a number called b{'the round'} is {'the binary'}.\n".
    a text called 'the character' is 'draw and record' of 'literal character'.
    'put' of "a number called c{'the round'} is {'the character'}.\n".
    a text called 'the boolean' is 'draw and record' of 'literal boolean'.
    'put' of "a boolean called w{'the round'} is {'the boolean'}.\n".
    a text called 'the text' is 'draw and record' of 'literal text'.
    'put' of "a text called t{'the round'} is {'the text'}.\n".
    a text called 'the absent value' is 'draw and record' of 'literal nothing'.
    'put' of "a list called l{'the round'} is [{'the absent value'}].\n".
    a text called 'the octal' is 'draw and record' of 'literal octal number'.
    'put' of "a number called o{'the round'} is {'the octal'}.\n".
    a text called 'the base conversion' is 'draw and record' of 'literal base conversion'.
    'put' of "a number called k{'the round'} is {'the base conversion'}.\n".
    a text called 'any number at all' is 'draw and record' of 'literal any number'.
    'put' of "a number called a{'the round'} is {'any number at all'}.\n".

(The format-string round. The holes name variables the program really
 has - LANGUAGE.md:3206 is explicit that a bare literal inside a hole is
 rejected, so the caller must supply names, which is why the helper takes
 a list. An empty list is exercised too: it must degrade to a plain text
 literal rather than error.)

To 'one round of format strings' with a number called 'the round'.
    a list called 'the names in scope' is ["n{'the round'}", "g{'the round'}", "t{'the round'}", "w{'the round'}"].
    a list called 'the kinds in scope' is ["number", "float", "text", "boolean"].
    a text called 'the format string' is 'draw and record' of 'literal format string' of 'the names in scope' and 'the kinds in scope'.
    'put' of "    a text called f{'the round'} is {'the format string'}.\n".
    a list called 'no names at all' is [].
    a list called 'no kinds at all' is [].
    a text called 'the plain one' is 'draw and record' of 'literal format string' of 'no names at all' and 'no kinds at all'.
    'put' of "    a text called p{'the round'} is {'the plain one'}.\n".

To 'build the corpus'.
    a number called 'the round' is 1.
    While 'the round' is less than or equal to 120,
        'one round of every helper' of 'the round',
        Set 'the round' to 'the round' add 1.
    'put' of "\nTo 'the format strings are never rendered'.\n".
    Set 'the round' to 1.
    While 'the round' is less than or equal to 120,
        'one round of format strings' of 'the round',
        Set 'the round' to 'the round' add 1.
    'put' of "\nExit 0.\n".

'rng seed' of 4242.
'build the corpus'.
a text called 'the first tape' is "{'the sample tape'}".

open a file for writing called 'the corpus file' at "./vf_literals.vox".
Write 'the corpus' to 'the corpus file'.
Close 'the corpus file'.

Print "min length: {'the shortest'} bytes".
Print "max length: {'the longest'} bytes".
a number called 'the mean length' is 'the total length' divide 'how many measured'.
Print "mean length: {'the mean length'} bytes over {'how many measured'} literals".
Print "longer than 12 bytes: {'how many over twelve'}".
Print "every literal is at least one byte: {'the shortest' is greater than 0}".
Print "lengths vary: {'the longest' is greater than 'the shortest'}".
Print "the tail is reachable: {'how many over twelve' is greater than 0}".

(Same seed, same literals.)
clear 'the corpus'.
clear 'the sample tape'.
'rng seed' of 4242.
'build the corpus'.
a text called 'the second tape' is "{'the sample tape'}".
Print "reproducible: {'the first tape' is equal to 'the second tape'}".

(The compiler comes from 'vox path', which honours $VOX the way test.sh
 sets it - a literal ../vox/... would only be right in a plain checkout
 and would silently skip the check in a worktree.)
a text called 'the compile command' is "{'shell quote' of 'vox path'} ./vf_literals.vox -o ./vf_literals > ./vf_literals.cerr 2>&1".
a number called 'the compile status' is 'run shell' of 'the compile command' and 120000.
Print "every literal compiles: {'the compile status' is 0}".
a number called 'the run status' is 'run shell' of "./vf_literals > ./vf_literals.out 2>&1" and 60000.
Print "the program runs: {'the run status' is 0}".

(Only on failure, so the expected file is unchanged when this passes.)
If 'the compile status' is not 0 then,
    a text called 'the compiler complaint' is 'read whole file' of "./vf_literals.cerr",
    Print "the compiler was {'vox path'}",
    Print 'the compiler complaint'.
If 'the run status' is not 0 then,
    a text called 'what the program printed' is 'read whole file' of "./vf_literals.out",
    Print 'what the program printed'.
